From bff3b85418717d28ff4370f24dc46a1a1d8a20c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (sandbox)" <claude@anthropic.local>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:48:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add Gate 6B feature ranking across the real 24 worlds

Two questions kept separate: what explains Gate 6's per-world outcome
(extends Gate 5B's eigenvalue/eigenvector hybrid-operator split from one
tree to all 24 worlds), and what predicts it in advance (Aizenbud-style
single-feature R^2 ranking of spectral gap, tree depth, port-sensor path
length, and initial alignment against adaptive_minus_frozen/shuffled).

Precondition checked before trusting anything downstream: regenerated
adaptive_minus_frozen and adaptive_minus_shuffled for all 24 worlds by
importing gate6_many_worlds' own functions verbatim, and required them to
match results/gate6.json to 0.0 exactly. reproduces_gate6_receipt in the
frozen output records that check so it can't silently drift.

Headline result: eigenvector-only hybrid gain explains R^2=0.989 of
adaptive_minus_frozen (0.852 vs shuffled), eigenvalue-only explains 0.302;
sign matches in 21/24 vs 13/24 worlds; holds excluding the three largest
swings. The pre-existing-structure ranking is much weaker -- spectral gap,
the closest analogue to Aizenbud's total dendritic area, gets R^2=0.002.
Best predictor is initial alignment alone (R^2=0.384); best two-feature
pair barely improves on that (0.451). Written up plainly in the
interpretation field rather than as a discovery it isn't.

76/76 tests green on this checkout (66 pre-existing + 10 new).
